artifact.self_contained_problem:
Define a social organization to be a (formal or informal) group of interrelated individuals pursuing a collective goal and embedded in an external environment that can deliver disruptive events. Associate to each such organization $O$ three time-dependent attributes:

(i) vulnerability $V(O,t)$, measuring the susceptibility of $O$ to disruptions in its environment;
(ii) anticipation $A(O,t)$, measuring the foresight or situational awareness $O$ has regarding its vulnerabilities;
(iii) adaptive capacity $C(O,t)$, measuring the flexibility or fluidity $O$ has in mitigating vulnerabilities or responding to disruptions once they occur.

Given a crisis (a disruptive shock delivered by the environment at some time $t_0$), say that $O$ is resilient if, after the crisis, the core capabilities of $O$ are preserved and possibly enhanced relative to their pre-crisis level, and say that $O$ fails otherwise.

Problem. Construct a mathematical model — for instance, a dynamical system, agent-based system, network model, or stochastic process — whose state encodes the attributes $V$, $A$, and $C$ and whose dynamics depend on the environment and on the internal structure of $O$, such that the model:

(a) explains, on the basis of $V$, $A$, $C$ and structural/environmental parameters, why certain organizations thrive and enhance core capabilities under crisis while structurally similar ones fail;
(b) yields, from $V(O,t_0)$, $A(O,t_0)$, $C(O,t_0)$ and crisis parameters, quantitative predictions of post-crisis core-capability levels that can be compared with empirical data on real organizations;
(c) identifies sharp, model-internal conditions on $(V,A,C)$ and on the organization's structure under which resilience (capability enhancement) versus failure occurs.

Provide such a model and rigorously analyze conditions (a)–(c).

